==Trivia==
*All of the Japanese stage names have a connection to math.
**[[Pursuit of Understanding]] is simply about finding solutions to equations.
***However, this stage's Japanese name is more of a reference to ''[[wikipedia:3000 Leagues in Search of Mother|3000 Leagues in Search of Mother]]'', a Japanese anime and movie.
**[[Pythagorean Tributary]] is a reference to the Greek mathematician and philosopher [[wikipedia:Pythagoras|Pythagoras]].
**[[1 + 2 = Tor]] is a pun on the name of a fictional mountain (イチタスニハ山, ''ichitasuniha san'') and a statement of addition: 一足す二は三 (''ichi tasu ni wa san'', one plus two equals three).
**[[Sine-Cosine Mine]] directly includes the names of the trigonometric functions [[wikipedia:Sine and cosine|sine and cosine]].
**[[Isosceles Valley]] is a reference to [[wikipedia:Isosceles triangle|Isosceles Triangles]]. It has a pun with the kanji 渓 meaning valley and 形 meaning shape (both read as ''kei'') in the Japanese word for an isosceles triangle, 二等辺三角形.
**[[The Farthest Point]] refers to 点P (point P), which is commonly known by Japanese math students in a type of math problem that asks for the area of a region traced by the motion of a point named P.
*Every sub-chapter from Demon of Deciliter Bay to [[Resort De La Cospa]] contains a stage suited to each of the [[Ultra Form]]s added in the same update.

